{"name":"Sunken Treasure","hex":"#cccf86","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sunken","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["past participle of sink"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Caused, by natural or unnatural means, to be depressed (lower than the surrounding area) or submerged","(of eyes or cheeks) Seeming to have fallen deeper back into the face due to tiredness, illness, or old age."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sunken"},{"term":"treasure","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A collection of valuable things; accumulated wealth; a stock of money, jewels, etc.","Anything greatly valued."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To consider to be precious; to value highly.","To store or stow in a safe place."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/treasure"}],"entries":[{"title":"Marine salvage","blocks":[{"text":"Marine salvage is the process of recovering a ship and its cargo after a shipwreck or other maritime casualty. Salvage may encompass towing, lifting a vessel, or effecting repairs to a ship. Salvors are normally paid for their efforts. However, protecting the coastal environment from oil spillages or other contaminants from a modern ship can also be a motivator, as oil, cargo, and other pollutants can easily leak from a wreck and in these instances, governments or authorities may organise the salvage."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/P%C3%A4%C3%A4stet%C3%B6%C3%B6d_merel_73_%2808%29.jpg/330px-P%C3%A4%C3%A4stet%C3%B6%C3%B6d_merel_73_%2808%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marine_salvage"},{"title":"Treasure","blocks":[{"text":"Treasure is a concentration of wealth — often originating from ancient history — that is considered lost and/or forgotten until rediscovered. Some jurisdictions legally define what constitutes treasure, such as in the British Treasure Act 1996."}],"ima